1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,238
What does "the feature is down" actually look like?

2
00:00:03,238 --> 00:00:07,995
Ask an engineer and they'll describe it precisely: errors spiking, latency climbing,

3
00:00:07,995 --> 00:00:09,354
a dashboard turning red.

4
00:00:09,354 --> 00:00:14,055
Every one of those signals exists because something stopped responding correctly at

5
00:00:14,055 --> 00:00:15,187
the transport level.

6
00:00:15,187 --> 00:00:19,038
An AI feature can fail without moving a single one of those numbers.

7
00:00:19,038 --> 00:00:23,738
It returns a response, quickly, with no error, that happens to be wrong, or unsafe,

8
00:00:23,738 --> 00:00:25,947
or quietly worse than it was yesterday.

9
00:00:25,947 --> 00:00:27,023
The server is fine.

10
00:00:27,023 --> 00:00:28,042
The feature isn't.

11
00:00:28,042 --> 00:00:32,403
Nothing in a traditional monitoring stack was built to notice the difference.

12
00:00:32,403 --> 00:00:36,933
That gap is what this lecture closes, and it's the last row on 8.1's table for a

13
00:00:36,933 --> 00:00:41,690
reason: everything else in this section is about preventing a specific kind of risk.

14
00:00:41,690 --> 00:00:45,315
This one is about what happens the day prevention wasn't enough.

15
00:00:45,315 --> 00:00:47,410
This isn't a hypothetical gap either.

16
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:51,997
It's the direct product of everything Section seven and this section have already

17
00:00:51,997 --> 00:00:56,584
taught you: a feature can pass its launch scorecard, clear its risk register, and

18
00:00:56,584 --> 00:01:01,115
still degrade quietly in production, the same way 7.5's margin trap hid inside a

19
00:01:01,115 --> 00:01:02,587
healthy-looking dashboard.

20
00:01:02,587 --> 00:01:04,286
Detection was never automatic.

21
00:01:04,286 --> 00:01:05,362
It has to be built.

22
00:01:05,912 --> 00:01:09,669
Read the bottom row, because it's the entire argument in one line.

23
00:01:09,669 --> 00:01:14,451
Existing monitoring genuinely does catch the left column, reliably, which is exactly

24
00:01:14,451 --> 00:01:18,721
why it's tempting to assume the same dashboards cover the right column too.

25
00:01:18,721 --> 00:01:23,218
They don't, and assuming they do is how a quality incident runs for days before

26
00:01:23,218 --> 00:01:24,072
anyone notices.

27
00:01:24,072 --> 00:01:28,227
The signal that actually catches the right column has to be about quality

28
00:01:28,227 --> 00:01:32,839
specifically: a spike in thumbs-down feedback, a jump in support escalations from

29
00:01:32,839 --> 00:01:37,564
this feature, a sampled review that finds several outputs failing 4.5's rubric in a

30
00:01:37,564 --> 00:01:37,791
row.

31
00:01:37,791 --> 00:01:40,125
None of these are infrastructure metrics.

32
00:01:40,125 --> 00:01:44,907
All of them require someone to have decided, in advance, that this is worth watching

33
00:01:44,907 --> 00:01:48,152
continuously, not just checking at launch and forgetting.

34
00:01:48,152 --> 00:01:51,625
Picture the refund agent one more time, well into production.

35
00:01:51,625 --> 00:01:56,350
Its error rate stays flat, its latency stays flat, every infrastructure graph looks

36
00:01:56,350 --> 00:01:58,285
exactly like it did on launch day.

37
00:01:58,285 --> 00:02:03,010
And yet a model update upstream, or a subtle shift in the kind of tickets arriving,

38
00:02:03,010 --> 00:02:07,337
could be quietly pushing it toward approving refunds it shouldn't, at a rate

39
00:02:07,337 --> 00:02:12,062
nobody's watching for, because nothing wired to page anyone was ever built to watch

40
00:02:12,062 --> 00:02:12,460
for it.

41
00:02:13,010 --> 00:02:17,439
Notice the good column's third row, because it's the one teams skip most often.

42
00:02:17,439 --> 00:02:21,925
A kill switch nobody has actually tested is a kill switch you're discovering the

43
00:02:21,925 --> 00:02:26,634
failure mode of during the incident itself, at the worst possible moment to learn it

44
00:02:26,634 --> 00:02:27,812
doesn't work cleanly.

45
00:02:27,812 --> 00:02:32,521
Test the fallback the way you'd test anything else that matters: before you need it,

46
00:02:32,521 --> 00:02:33,138
not during.

47
00:02:33,138 --> 00:02:37,623
Turn the feature off, or revert it to a simpler, safer version, as a drill, on a

48
00:02:37,623 --> 00:02:40,090
quiet Tuesday, and fix whatever breaks then.

49
00:02:40,090 --> 00:02:44,127
The incident is a bad time to discover the kill switch itself has a bug.

50
00:02:44,127 --> 00:02:48,388
A named owner matters here for the same reason 8.1 insisted on one for every

51
00:02:48,388 --> 00:02:49,117
register row.

52
00:02:49,117 --> 00:02:53,322
"The on-call rotation will catch it" answers a different question than "who

53
00:02:53,322 --> 00:02:57,808
specifically reviews the quality-alert queue." The first is about infrastructure

54
00:02:57,808 --> 00:02:58,200
paging.

55
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:02,573
The second is a role nobody fills by default, and it stays empty until someone

56
00:03:02,573 --> 00:03:03,919
deliberately assigns it.

57
00:03:03,919 --> 00:03:07,563
Test the kill switch on a schedule, too, not just once at launch.

58
00:03:07,563 --> 00:03:12,217
A fallback verified working six months ago, against a version of the feature that's

59
00:03:12,217 --> 00:03:16,422
since changed twice, is a fact about the past, not a guarantee about today.

60
00:03:16,972 --> 00:03:21,779
Say the honest caveat plainly, because a well-written postmortem feels like closure,

61
00:03:21,779 --> 00:03:26,301
and it can quietly be the end of the learning instead of the useful part of it.

62
00:03:26,301 --> 00:03:30,650
A document explaining what went wrong helps the people in the room that day.

63
00:03:30,650 --> 00:03:35,458
A new golden-set case, built from the exact input that triggered the incident, helps

64
00:03:35,458 --> 00:03:40,265
every single build after it, forever, automatically, every time the regression suite

65
00:03:40,265 --> 00:03:40,552
runs.

66
00:03:40,552 --> 00:03:41,696
One of these scales.

67
00:03:41,696 --> 00:03:43,985
The other one is read once and archived.

68
00:03:43,985 --> 00:03:48,621
Close every incident the same way: the specific failing input becomes a permanent

69
00:03:48,621 --> 00:03:53,371
eval case before the postmortem doc is considered finished, not as a follow-up task

70
00:03:53,371 --> 00:03:54,917
that quietly never happens.

71
00:03:54,917 --> 00:03:59,324
This is also the fastest way this section's tools compound instead of sitting

72
00:03:59,324 --> 00:03:59,953
separately.

73
00:03:59,953 --> 00:04:04,646
A stronger golden set makes 7.4's scorecard more trustworthy the next time a model

74
00:04:04,646 --> 00:04:05,676
gets re-evaluated.

75
00:04:05,676 --> 00:04:10,369
A wider fairness slice, discovered through an incident, makes 8.5's segmented pass

76
00:04:10,369 --> 00:04:13,116
rate catch more next quarter than it does today.

77
00:04:13,116 --> 00:04:17,867
Nothing in this course is really separate once it's actually running in production.

78
00:04:18,417 --> 00:04:23,154
That's the standard worth building toward, and the example question is the one worth

79
00:04:23,154 --> 00:04:27,778
asking in the next team meeting, out loud, because most people have never actually

80
00:04:27,778 --> 00:04:29,187
tested the honest answer.

81
00:04:29,187 --> 00:04:33,586
Most teams discover, asking this for the first time, that their real detection

82
00:04:33,586 --> 00:04:38,266
mechanism is a user complaining loudly enough, which is detection, technically, but

83
00:04:38,266 --> 00:04:41,030
the slowest and most expensive version available.

84
00:04:41,030 --> 00:04:45,710
Ask it about the feature you're least confident in, not the one you're proudest of.

85
00:04:45,710 --> 00:04:47,797
The proud one already gets attention.

86
00:04:47,797 --> 00:04:52,308
The quietly-shipped one, the internal tool nobody's watching closely, is exactly

87
00:04:52,308 --> 00:04:56,707
where a quality incident runs longest before anyone notices, precisely because

88
00:04:56,707 --> 00:04:59,413
nobody built a signal for it in the first place.

89
00:04:59,963 --> 00:05:01,550
One thing before you move on.

90
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:06,092
Check the detection question honestly, the same exercise as every register row this

91
00:05:06,092 --> 00:05:08,718
section has asked for: specific, not reassuring.

92
00:05:08,718 --> 00:05:13,150
Then test the kill switch, for real, on purpose, before an incident forces you to

93
00:05:13,150 --> 00:05:14,409
test it under pressure.

94
00:05:14,409 --> 00:05:18,895
If it doesn't exist yet, that's today's finding, and it's a better one to have now

95
00:05:18,895 --> 00:05:21,631
than at two in the morning during a real incident.

96
00:05:21,631 --> 00:05:25,844
Both of these belong on the same afternoon as 8.1's first register row, not a

97
00:05:25,844 --> 00:05:27,486
separate initiative for later.

98
00:05:27,486 --> 00:05:31,973
An incident plan with no detection signal and no tested kill switch isn't a lesser

99
00:05:31,973 --> 00:05:33,231
version of a real plan.

100
00:05:33,231 --> 00:05:37,608
It's the appearance of one, worth naming honestly rather than assuming it covers

101
00:05:37,608 --> 00:05:37,827
you.

102
00:05:37,827 --> 00:05:42,423
Next lecture is this section's workshop: red-team your own feature, using everything

103
00:05:42,423 --> 00:05:46,637
from the risk register through today's incident plan, against something you'd

104
00:05:46,637 --> 00:05:47,403
actually ship.
